Filament Tumbao
Ingredients: TIMBA + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Flute  
Creative Catalyst: Its first record was cut aboard a generation ship halfway to Proxima Centauri.

Filament Tumbao is the sound of hyper-syncopated Afro-Cuban polyrhythms colliding head-on with claustrophobic, life-support-failure industrial grime. Blown through cracked wooden and pressurized titanium flutes, the music matches the manic, aggressive bass tumbaos of Havana's hardest dancefloors with the screech of dying cooling pumps and high-pressure steam releases. It is blistering, sweat-drenched dance music engineered by and for generations trapped inside a flying steel coffin.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 104–116 BPM. The pulse is built on an aggressive, off-kilter 3:2 rumba clave re-metered by the ship's 0.6G artificial rotation, creating a lurching, heavy-footed polyrhythmic drive that stutters without ever losing its frantic momentum.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark Cuban montunos in Phrygian and Locrian modes, warped by microtonal tape-drift and cold, synthetic minor ninths that mimic malfunctioning engine klaxons.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Airlock Gating: Extreme side-chain compression keyed directly to simulated room-decompression bursts, sucking the air out of the mix before snapping back.
  2. Condensation Reverb: Convolution reverbs captured within hollow aluminum bulkheads and dripping hydroponic piping.
  3. Contact-Transduced Flute Overdrive: Flutes fitted with piezoelectric throat-mics and industrial contact sensors, run hot through bitcrushers until breath sounds register as searing white noise.
- Dynamics: Abrupt transitions between breathless, hypoxic density—where congas, synths, and flutes battle for frequency space—and horrifying vacuum drops where every sound cut-outs instantly, leaving only a lone woodwind gasping over hull creaks before a violent bloque (timba break) slams everything back into motion.

Scene & Origin Lore
Filament Tumbao was born in the service gutters of the Hab-Arship Mariana Grajales during Year 42 of its century-long transit to Proxima Centauri. Cut off from Earth and facing severe resource rationing, the ship's maintenance underclass—the "Henequeneros"—weaponized scrap copper wiring, decommissioned coolant pipes, and failing air-filtration cyclers into acoustic salvage. Denied access to private comms, they gathered in the lower machine decks at an illegal venue dubbed El Telar (The Loom). Fashion in the scene became an act of visceral survival: stripped pressurized inner-skins heavily embroidered with recycled copper yarn, respirators fitted with vibration-sensing acoustic reeds, and thick rubber work boots fitted with steel toe-caps to hammer syncopated accents directly into the deckplates. Dancing this music is a communal lung workout and an act of mutinous vitality; the crowd moves in dense, interlocking rings (a zero-gravity evolution of rueda de casino) to generate kinetic energy and defy the dead silence of deep space.

Typical Instruments
- Pressurized Charanga Flute: Traditional wooden 5-key and custom carbon-fiber flutes equipped with pneumatic bypass valves and internal throat-contact pickups.
- Freon-Tank Congas: Salvaged chemical containers fitted with vulcanized synthetic membranes and tuned to bone-snapping pitches.
- Circuit-Bent Soviet Montuno Rig: Rebuilt analog keyboard modules playing algorithmic, dizzying piano montunos run through rust-distorted preamps.
- Floor-Exciter Electric Baby Bass: An upright electric sub-bass wired directly to low-frequency structural shakers bolted to the ship's floor.
- Ball-Bearing Güiro & Shekere: Pneumatic conduits and scrap metal containers scraped and shaken with loose steel bearings.

For Fans Of: Los Van Van, Blanck Mass, Shabaka Hutchings, clipping.

